        "body": "For offshore program owners and operations leaders, the boundary is only real if it is visible in the pipeline, not just in a policy file. A analyst who guesses at offshore onboarding turns a small gap into an escalation. The boundary should be written in the same place the work is assigned. A analyst who cannot see the boundary will invent one under pressure. In the where the analyst’s work stops step, the written action is to record each onboarding task, the owner, and the completion date.\n\nThe pipeline should name the manager who decides any decision about access level, probation, or employment terms, not just the department. Escalation is a normal step, not a failure of the offshore onboarding process. When the boundary moves, tell the analyst on the same day it changes.\n\nThe onboarding record entry should show that an escalation happened and who received it. A written boundary protects the client relationship as much as the analyst. Keep the offshore onboarding scope narrow enough that one person can actually own it."
