        "body": "The offshore onboarding review should look at the oldest resolution, not only the newest. A request that needed a correction is useful proof about the routine. The onboarding record entry should distinguish a confirmed resolution from an assumed one. For offshore program owners and operations leaders, a defensible resolution is worth more than a fast one. In the how to confirm and resolve step, the written action is to confirm access only after the approved checks are attached.\n\nKeep the resolution rule fixed for the review window so the comparison means something. Close the onboarding record only after the destination entry shows the approved change. A second pair of eyes is worth the time on any offshore onboarding exception. The handoff note stays with the case file and the next checkpoint date.\n\nThe resolution note should say what was confirmed and by whom. Reopened requests are a useful signal about where the routine is weak. The offshore onboarding resolution rule should stay stable across the review window."
