        "body": "If the escalation trigger boundary lives only in a training call, it disappears the first busy week. The client owner keeps the decisions that move money, access, policy, or a client promise. Write down which systems the analyst may touch and which questions go back to the client owner. A prepared escalation trigger packet is a question waiting for a decision, not an approval in itself. In the where the analyst’s work stops step, the written action is to record the trigger, the threshold, and the person notified.\n\nThe anything touching safety, legal exposure, or a client commitment stays with the accountable owner, however complete the preparation looks. The analyst can assemble proof and flag a gap; the analyst cannot decide the exception. When the escalation trigger rule changes, apply the new rule to later requests and leave earlier classifications intact.\n\nThe test is whether the client owner can reopen the request next month without calling the original analyst. A boundary that is never written down becomes a different boundary for each new hire. The {pipeline} should route anything touching safety, legal exposure, or a client commitment to a named owner instead of parking it in the general list."
