        "body": "A five-minute end-of-shift note prevents most next-morning confusion. The {pipeline} should be cleared of duplicate entries before new work starts. Log the exception where the next person will look, not in a private note. A routine that fits on one page is more likely to survive a busy week. In the the daily client communication routine step, the written action is to flag any message that differs from the approved summary.\n\nThe analyst should leave each client update with a date and a next step. Review the client communication list before accepting a new request, not after. A consistent daily start time makes the pipeline easier to trust.\n\nThe daily client communication routine is short: open the list, confirm the origin, log the exception, and leave a named next action. Start with the oldest unresolved item, because age turns a small gap into an escalation. Small habits carry client communication: date the origin, initial the check, state the next action, and never copy a familiar value. The onboarding pipeline carries the task owner, the proof, and the access date."
