        "body": "Review the offshore capacity list before accepting a new request, not after. A consistent daily start time makes the pipeline easier to trust. The daily offshore capacity routine is short: open the list, confirm the origin, log the exception, and leave a named next action. Start with the oldest unresolved item, because age turns a small gap into an escalation. In the the daily offshore capacity routine step, the written action is to compare the trend with staffing and handoff changes.\n\nSmall habits carry offshore capacity: date the origin, initial the check, state the next action, and never copy a familiar value. Keep the {pipeline} small enough to finish, because a healthy average can hide one request that sat for weeks. End each shift with a dated note that names what moved, what stalled, and who checks next. For offshore programs, the queue entry and the client update must agree before the check.\n\nWrite the offshore capacity rule where the work happens, not in a document nobody opens. The routine should make the next owner faster, not just busier. A daily list with ten open items is easier to trust than a dashboard with a hundred. The handoff note stays with the case file and the next checkpoint date."
